## Context

Init never overwrites, so canonical doctrine drifts away from what a host installed. Upgrade closes the gap, and it touches two kinds of host artifact that need different handling. Methodology files belong to the package: the running server's copy is the correct one, so the only question is whether to take it. The aide server entry is judged against the global launch, AIDE's canonical form, so a launch through the package runner is drift. A pinned project reaches a server that can switch it only by first moving its pin, and an old server cannot know newer releases, so that move is judged against a target version the caller names. The brain config belongs to the user: it holds wiring and customizations no canonical copy knows about, and it drifts in three separate ways — sections the contract now requires but the file lacks, shipped sections whose text is an old template version, and a launcher in a legacy shape.

Three parties are involved and each can do only part of the work. The tool holds its own version's canonical bytes and can write host files. The agent can talk to the user but cannot be trusted to reproduce canonical text it was handed. The user is the only one who can approve a change to anything they own. Under the source tree's rule, only install, sync and a consent-gated upgrade may write host files, so the brain config's repairs have to come through here, gated by consent.

The end-to-end brain repair order, and the reason tool responses stay small, are set out in the brain provisioning spec. This spec covers only what upgrade decides inside that flow.

## Strategy

**Detecting drift and applying it are separate calls.** A detection call reads every category, writes nothing and returns status, counts and names only. Applying a change is a separate call that names one category. The user's decision sits between the two calls, so no single invocation can both find drift and act on it, and a detection pass has nothing to apply. The agent shows each category as its own decision; nothing upgrades the whole surface on one yes.

**For package-owned categories the category is the unit, and the tool does the writing.** On a confirmed category the tool writes every differing or missing file from canonical and returns a manifest. Half a category would leave artifacts referencing each other at mismatched versions. The agent never receives canonical bytes, so it cannot paraphrase or invent them.

**The aide entry moves to global only after the package is installed.** On the user's yes the agent installs the package globally, and the tool rewrites the entry to the bare global command only once that install is confirmed, refusing otherwise: an entry naming a command the machine lacks leaves the next session with no aide server. Only that entry changes, with no machine-specific path, and a correct global entry is never drift. The brain launcher moves through the brain config flow.

**A pin move is the one-time transit, and the caller names its target.** The pinned server predates the switch to global and cannot perform it, so it first moves to the latest release, whose upgrade then offers the switch. The boot report knows the latest and passes it; only an exact published version is accepted. On a yes the tool changes only the version, keeping the command form and every other byte, since the agent never hand-edits the MCP config. The new pin runs in a new session.

**The brain config is written only with consent and only by insertion.** A yes to the brain category inserts every missing section and touches nothing already there, because the user approved additions, not edits to text they own. Inserted text takes the file's line endings, since a mixed file looks fully rewritten in diffs. The manifest lists only the inserted markers, zero template bytes, and a re-run with nothing missing returns it empty, so an interrupted repair is safe to retry.

**Template text is inserted only into the template's own integration.** The bundled template is written for Obsidian. Before inserting, the tool checks the config's integration. If it is Obsidian, the sections go in byte-identical. If it is anything else, the tool inserts nothing and returns the names of the sections the user must adapt with the agent. Pasting Obsidian instructions into a config for another backend would satisfy the parser and still mislead every agent that reads it later.

**Inside the brain config, each outdated section is its own decision, guarded by its exact bytes.** Stale sections and a legacy launcher sit outside the category yes; each is shown as its own before-and-after change and applied alone. The apply carries a byte-exact fingerprint of the before text the user saw; any change since is refused with an instruction to re-fetch the diff, because a whitespace-tolerant match would overwrite an edit made after review. Only text matching a known outdated template version is offered, so customized sections never are.

## Good examples

The user approves the agents category, which has three files that differ and one that is missing. The tool writes all four and returns their names. The agent reports "four agent files updated" without ever having seen what is in them.

A brain config is missing two sections and has one stale section. The user approves the brain category. One call inserts both sections in the file's existing line endings and returns two markers. The stale section is offered separately as a small diff, the user says no, and it stays as it is. A second run of the brain category returns an empty manifest.

The boot report says a pinned project is three releases behind and names the latest. The user says yes, the upgrade call names that version, and the tool changes only the version; the wrapped Windows launch and the brain server's entry are byte-identical. In the new session the upgrade reports the runner launch as drift, the user says yes, the agent installs the package globally, and the tool rewrites the aide entry to the bare command, every other byte unchanged.

## Bad examples

A yes to the brain category that rewrites the config from the template "to bring it fully current." Every missing section appears, but the user's launcher wiring and an edited section are replaced without their approval.

An insertion manifest that echoes each inserted block "so the agent can confirm it." Tests pass, and the response grows by the full template size on every repair, which is the bloat the flow exists to remove.

A pin move that writes a floating latest tag "so it never falls behind again," or a switch that writes the bare global command before the global install has succeeded. Today's session works and the tests pass, but the next one either resolves whatever was published that morning, with files that no longer match the running version, or finds no aide server to launch.

